  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "If we let $u=2x$, then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing our limits of integration, we get the following:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5$ |\n\nThen, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({e}^{u}){|}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}({e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot ({5}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
